# Containerfile.broker — THE BASE BROKER IMAGE, and the local/Mac arm's broker box
# (#98 two-box topology).
#
# Despite living under arms/local, this is not a local-only prototype: it is the base
# of the whole consumer-broker image lineage (docs/broker-image-cutover.md). Consumers
# build `FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}` and add only their own manifests/connectors —
# examples/restricted_mcp_server, examples/alpaca_paper_drill, and a consumer agent's,
# the last of which is what PRODUCTION runs. Anything changed here reaches all of them, so
# changes must stay additive unless a consumer rebuild is being coordinated.
#
# Build from the REPO ROOT (it installs the safe-agents SDK + COPYs the model-proxy stub):
#   podman build -t safe-agents-local-broker:dev -f safe_agents/arms/local/Containerfile.broker .
#
# One broker process serving the two surfaces the confined agent reaches over the network:
#   :8443  model-inference forward proxy (allowlist api.anthropic.com)  [needs NAT egress]
#   :8080  brokered tool-call API (the BrokerRuntime)
#
# The backends are env-selected, not baked (broker_server.py's three switches): the
# memory arm keeps in-memory fakes for a smoke, and the durable arms take a real store,
# audit tape and credential provider. The entrypoint starts BOTH surfaces; a deployment
# that wants only the tool API overrides the command.
FROM python:3.12-slim

# Install the safe-agents SDK (sa#106) so `safe_agents.broker.*` resolves as an installed
# package — a single source of truth for the runtime deps (pydantic, PyYAML) via pyproject,
# rather than hand-maintained pins. The `aws` extra adds boto3, needed only when the stores are
# DynamoDB-backed (BROKER_STORE=dynamo); boto3 stays lazy-imported in the broker library.
WORKDIR /app
COPY pyproject.toml README.md ./
COPY safe_agents ./safe_agents
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir ".[aws]"

# The model-proxy stub runs as a standalone script (not a module), so copy it to a fixed path.
COPY safe_agents/arms/ec2/bootstrap/scripts/model-proxy-stub.py /app/model-proxy-stub.py
COPY safe_agents/arms/local/broker-entrypoint.sh /app/broker-entrypoint.sh

# --- Arbitrary-UID ("SCC-safe") hardening (#249) -----------------------------
# OpenShift's default SCC IGNORES `USER` and runs the container as a random high
# UID from the namespace's range, in GID 0, with no writable $HOME. So "non-root"
# is not the property that matters — "works as an ARBITRARY UID" is, and this
# image already had a non-root user while being broken for exactly that reason.
# That is the trap: it looks solved.
#
# PROVENANCE — OBSERVED as of 2026-07-27, on OpenShift 4.20.29. This image was
# built by an in-cluster BuildConfig and run under `restricted-v2` (annotation
# checked, not assumed); the platform assigned `runAsUser=1000850000`,
# `fsGroup=1000850000`, `runAsGroup` unset so the primary gid falls to 0. $HOME
# was writable, /app stayed read-only, the projected secrets mount was readable,
# and the ceremony arc ran to an executed brokered call
# (safe_agents/arms/openshift/, #250 Phase 2). The paragraph above is no longer
# a documentation claim.
#
# TWO THINGS THE EMULATION GOT WRONG, both discovered by that first real pod:
#
#   * A random UID DOES get an /etc/passwd entry. CRI-O synthesizes one, exactly
#     as podman does — `1000850000:x:1000850000:0:1000850000 user:/home/broker:
#     /sbin/nologin`. The podman drill's `--passwd=false` therefore makes the
#     drill STRICTER than the platform rather than more faithful to it. Harmless
#     as a test posture (running without an entry is a superset), but the
#     rationale was false.
#   * Consequently the ceremony identity does NOT degrade to
#     `unknown-user@<host>`: `getpass.getuser()` resolves through the synthesized
#     entry and the records read `local-solo:1000850000@<pod-name>#maker`. The
#     note further down about that degradation describes the podman drill, not a
#     pod.
#
# Which relocates why the $HOME hardening below is load-bearing. It is not that
# the passwd lookup FAILS — it succeeds. It is that the synthesized entry's home
# directory is whatever `ENV HOME` says, and on an image without one it is `/`,
# which an arbitrary UID cannot write. Setting HOME explicitly is what makes the
# lookup land somewhere real.
#
# The `broker` user and group STAY, and the hardening is purely additive. Three
# consumer images build FROM this base and every one does `COPY
# --chown=broker:broker` + `USER broker` — examples/restricted_mcp_server,
# examples/alpaca_paper_drill, and a consumer agent's, which is what PRODUCTION runs
# (docs/broker-image-cutover.md). Dropping the user to satisfy the SCC would be a
# cross-repo break for no gain; both execution modes work as written below.
#
# Two things an arbitrary UID needs and this image did not give it:
#
#   * Group 0 must be able to write $HOME. OpenShift puts the random UID in GID 0,
#     so root-group ownership plus group-write is the standard way to hand it a
#     writable directory. /app is deliberately NOT made writable — application code
#     the runtime user can rewrite is a posture regression, and nothing writes there
#     (the sqlite db and the audit tape are both named-or-refuse and mounted).
#   * HOME must not depend on where a passwd lookup POINTS. Setting it explicitly
#     to the same path the `broker` user already gets keeps the podman run
#     byte-for-byte and gives the arbitrary UID a real directory — and, as the
#     first real pod showed, is also what the runtime-synthesized passwd entry
#     inherits as its home field.
#
# NB under the podman drill's `--passwd=false` the ceremony's OS-derived operator
# half degrades to `unknown-user@<host>` (`ceremony_identity._derive_operator`
# survives a missing passwd entry). On a real pod it does not degrade — see the
# provenance note above. Either way this is left ALONE deliberately: baking
# LOGNAME=broker would make getpass.getuser() report an operator the process is
# not running as, putting a false name in a signed ledger record. maker != checker
# compares ROLES, not this string, so the security property is unaffected.
RUN useradd -m broker \
    && usermod -aG 0 broker \
    && chgrp -R 0 /home/broker \
    && chmod -R g=u /home/broker
ENV HOME=/home/broker
USER broker
EXPOSE 8080 8443
ENTRYPOINT ["bash", "/app/broker-entrypoint.sh"]
